WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.040
What do you see when you think of our universe?

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.400
Whatever it might be...

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.400
...that’s just the start of the story 

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.680
 The stuff of matter, the stuff that we’re made of... 

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:15.880
 ...is not the whole story  

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.120
 As scientists have measured... 

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.760
 ...the way that the galaxies move around... 

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.080
 ...the way that the universe is actually expanding... 

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.600
 ...they realised that they’re not seeing most of the universe  

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.320
So what makes up everything else?

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.920
This could well be the biggest mystery of all 

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.720
 For a long time when people looked up into space... 

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.400
 ...they thought that everything in the universe...  

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.800
 ...stars, galaxies, planets, us were made of the same thing ...   

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.160
 ...which is atoms and molecules that contain protons and neutrons...  

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.000
 ...but as more data comes in, there are lots of anomalies  

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.400
 There’s actually much, much more other stuff out there 

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.200
Normal matter, the stuff we can see or detect...

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.280
...makes up just 5% of the composition of the universe 

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.440
Scientists think that the rest of the universe...

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:20.000
...is composed of two   other things: dark matter and dark energy 

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.560
So far, so good. But there’s a problem 

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.040
 Dark matter and dark energy are words for stuff that scientists don’t... 

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.440
 ...really understand, they’re kind of like metaphors... 

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.200
 ...for ignorance about the universe  

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.800
 All we know is that we can’t see either of them... 

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.560
 ...and they’re very hard to detect  

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.280
Neither dark matter nor dark energy absorb or emit light...

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.760
...making them completely invisible to modern scientific instruments

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.080
So the obvious question is how do scientists even know they exist?

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.520
 Dark matter cannot be detected by traditional space... 

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.680
 ...observatories or telescopes on the ground because all of those machines... 

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.600
 ...use electromagnetic radiation—light, basically—at different frequencies...  

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.400
 ...and dark matter doesn’t emit that kind of radiation  

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.440
 The only way that we know dark matter exists is by its gravitational effects  

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.160
These effects were first observed in 1933 by the astronomer Fritz Zwicky

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.240
While studying a distant cluster of galaxies...

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.760
...he and his team noticed something very strange

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.120
 They realised that the galaxies on the outer rim were moving way too fast... 

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.240
 ...there wasn’t enough gravitational force within that galaxy cluster... 

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.320
 ...to keep these galaxies at the edge  

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.760
 If these galaxies at the edge are moving that fast... 

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.920
 ...they should have flown away, but they weren’t flying away  

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.400
Zwicky and his team wrote that there must be something else present...

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.640
...some missing mass—holding the galaxies together 

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:57.240
 The overall name for this missing mass that we can’t see is dark matter ...   

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.280
...and  that missing mass was observed in lots of other places  

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.520
The presence of dark matter seems to act like an invisible glue...

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.320
...holding the normal matter in the universe together 

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.680
But finding out what it actually is...

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.480
...is like trying to solve a detective mystery 

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.920
 Any questions? All right, you all have your instructions , let’s go  

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.040
You have to identify the likely suspects

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.880
First, there are MACHOs...

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.040
...Massive Astrophysical Compact Halo Objects

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.800
These are mostly dead or failed stars like neutron stars...

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.560
...which are leftover when a massive star runs out of fuel and collapses 

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.320
Or brown dwarfs, which are enormous clumps of atoms...

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.840
...that never quite managed to develop into stars 

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.640
These sound promising at first, but nowadays...

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.000
...many scientists think that MACHOs are unlikey to be behind dark matter

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.440
So maybe it’s WIMPs

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.760
But, Weakly Interacting Massive Particles are just one of the hypothetical particles...

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.600
...that scientists are actually looking for

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.560
There are a dizzying array of other potential suspects...

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.720
...many of which are also yet to be discovered in practice

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.280
Little wonder then, that many eyes turned...

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.480
...to the Large Hadron Collider at CERN in Geneva 

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:30.040
Scientists there have been searching for possible dark matter particles...

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.240
...for more than a decade 

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.720
But they’re yet to find anything...

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.160
...so now what?

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:41.160
 As well as looking in particle colliders to make the particles of dark matter... 

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:47.880
 ...another way is to look out into space and look for the gravitational effects of it  

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:52.640
 There’s a new class of instruments called gravitational-wave detection machines... 

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.680
 ...which really only started working a few years ago on Earth  

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.280
 These can detect the ripples in space-time that gravitational objects create  

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.640
 If there’s huge clumps of dark matter somewhere doing something interesting... 

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:06.600
 ...they will potentially emit gravitational waves... 

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.240
 ...and that’s another way in to finding out what they might be  

