 For the best offer, click the link... 

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.960
 ...and now back to the film 

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.800
If you think dark matter is a challenging mystery to unravel...

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.880
...that’s nothing compared to dark energy 

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:47.920
Dark energy is similar to dark matter in just a couple of ways 

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.240
It’s invisible and it has the word dark in it 

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.080
Beyond that, well, scientists are struggling...

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:00.000
...but they think it exists because of one incredible discovery 

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.440
Ever since the Big Bang, the universe has been expanding 

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.640
Scientists used to believe that the pull of gravity...

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.280
...would eventually cause its expansion to slow down 

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:15.200
But in the late 1990s, they actually found the opposite 

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.680
The expansion of the universe was speeding up 

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.800
This finding was extraordinary 

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.640
Imagine you threw a ball into the air...

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.080
...and instead of being pulled back down via gravity...

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.960
...it just continues to fly straight up into the sky 

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:32.880
Well, the discovery of dark energy was kind of the same thing 

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.240
It had scientists stumped 

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.320
 Now, this was a big surprise and couldn’t be explained... 

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.840
 ...using the physics that astrophysicists already had at the time  

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:49.320
 So as a placeholder to try and explain why the universe is not only expanding... 

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.840
 ...but that expansion is accelerating...  

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.520
 ...they created this stuff called dark energy  

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:57.640
Scientists think that dark energy accounts...

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.680
...for the majority of the energy in the universe...

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.720
...but most of its properties still remain a mystery 

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.880
 Dark energy really is beyond any theories of established physics right now  

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:13.360
 All we know about it is that it has the opposite effect of gravity  

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.800
 Gravity pulls things together and dark energy pushes things apart  

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:20.560
 Now, we don’t know if it’s a particle or a fluid or a force... 

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.880
 ...that’s pushing things around everywhere... 

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.320
 ...or whether it’s a property of space itself  

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:33.520
One theory is that dark energy could be something like a fluid that fills up space 

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.400
Some theorists have call it “quintessence”

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.760
Another possibility takes us back in time...

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.960
...to one of the fathers of modern physics

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:49.640
Albert Einstein realised that empty space isn’t simply nothing 

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.920
He argued that there must be a force at play there...

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:57.240
...to counteract gravity, otherwise the universe would just collapse

00:07:57.880 --> 00:07:59.200
To account for this force... 

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:04.760
...he added a term called the cosmological constant to his theory of gravity 

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:09.760
Einstein believed that the universe was neither expanding nor contracting

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.880
When it was later discovered that the universe is expanding...

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:17.520
...he discarded the idea of a cosmological constant 

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.880
He later called it his biggest blunder 

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.520
But what if it wasn’t?

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:30.720
 The thing is, as we discover what dark energy is, it seems to quite neatly match... 

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.760
 ...with the idea of a cosmological constant  

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.520
 So some people are trying to introduce the idea of a cosmological constant... 

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.120
 ...back into general relativity... 

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.640
 ...to try and explain some of the things that dark energy does... 

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.720
 ...so Einstein thought he was wrong, but he might have been right  

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:50.120
The cosmological constant or something like it...

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:54.560
...could be the force driving the expansion of the universe 

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.360
Whichever theory ultimately explains dark energy...

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.880
...unravelling its mystery and working out how strong its force really is...

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.560
...may give us a glimpse into the future of our universe

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.640
Maybe dark energy will keep pushing galaxies away from each other...

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:18.920
...until they become lonely dots in the vast emptiness of space 

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:23.280
The stars would stop shining, everything would go cold...

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.440
...and the universe will face what’s called the Big Freeze 

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.600
Or perhaps we’re heading for the equally terrifying Big Rip...

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.560
...where dark energy overcomes gravity and rips the universe apart 

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.400
 If you want to know where the universe is going... 

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.480
 ...you need to understand dark energy, because that’s the force ...

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:49.000
 ...or energy at the most cosmic scale that is shaping the structure of the universe  

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:53.240
In the meantime, scientists will continue their efforts...

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:57.200
...to solve the mysteries of dark energy, dark matter...

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:59.960
...and the universe itself 

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.720
 Understanding these phenomena are really important... 

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:05.960
 ...for scientists to get a grip on what the universe actually is now... 

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:06.920
 ...to understand the Big Bang... 

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:09.920
 ...but also what’s going to happen far off into the future  

